<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Didrikson Zaharias, now 42, comes for her yearly preventive visit on the morning of her seventh dose of dostarlimab. Six weeks ago, at the restaging visit, the tumor had almost gone on MRI and endoscopy, and levothyroxine 75 micrograms was started for immune-related hypothyroidism. She takes it on waking, four hours apart from her iron, and the tiredness and cold hands have gone. Her bowel movements are normal, with no blood, urgency or pain. She is playing 18 holes most days and finished third in a regional event last week. She has had no diarrhea, rash, cough or breathlessness since the cold in June. Her PHQ-9 today is 2. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 42</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Mismatch repair-deficient low rectal cancer on dostarlimab with a near-complete response; immune-related hypothyroidism on levothyroxine; iron deficiency anemia, recovering</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Dostarlimab every three weeks; levothyroxine 75 micrograms daily; ferrous sulfate 325 mg on alternate days; levonorgestrel intrauterine device</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-9 2</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on a tablet in the waiting room and reviewed with her by Dr. Vasquez-Thornbury. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Very good</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Busy with tournaments and the club; worried at times that the cancer will come back; sleeps well</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never smoked</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol and drugs</td><td>A drink once or twice a month; no drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Golf most days, walking the course; strength work three times a week</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Has cut red meat to twice a week and eats more vegetables and whole grains</td></tr><tr><td>Sun exposure</td><td>Outdoors most of the day; uses sunscreen inconsistently; wears a visor</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>Sex comfortable again since the bleeding stopped; levonorgestrel IUD in place; not planning a pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>Safety at home</td><td>Feels safe; no one hurts, threatens or controls her; no firearm in the home</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt; drives long distances between tournaments and stops to rest</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

