    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Bright red blood with bowel movements for about three months, with narrower stools, urgency, a feeling of incomplete emptying, and a firm, ulcerated mass felt on the back wall of the lower rectum at about 5 cm from the anal verge: a rectal cancer must be confirmed or excluded. Colonoscopy with biopsy booked this week</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K62.5</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to the documented fatigue before her cancer diagnosis. Superseded by the diagnosis at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Iron deficiency anemia from chronic rectal blood loss: hemoglobin 11.0 g/dL, MCV 77 fL, ferritin 9 ng/mL, with tiredness</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>D50.0</td><td>S — Synthetic finding anchored to the documented fatigue at the end of the 1952 season.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Adenocarcinoma of the low rectum, mismatch repair deficient (loss of MSH2 and MSH6; microsatellite instability high), cT3bN1M0 (stage IIIB) on pelvic MRI, lower edge 4 cm from the anal verge and about 1 cm above the anorectal junction, mesorectal fascia clear, no extramural venous invasion; CEA 5.8 ng/mL; synthetic modern detail linked to her documented cancer, whose site the sources do not record exactly</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C20</td><td>S — Synthetic histology, site, stage, mismatch repair status and course; the sources say she had cancer treated with a colostomy and call it colon cancer (R on the site). See sources.md.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Acute viral upper respiratory infection with cough and sore throat, a week before cycle 4 of dostarlimab; oxygen saturation and chest X-ray normal, so immune-related pneumonitis is unlikely; rapid influenza and COVID-19 tests negative; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J06.9</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent illness; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Immune-related primary hypothyroidism from dostarlimab, grade 2: tiredness and cold hands, TSH 12.6 mIU/L and free T4 0.6 ng/dL after four doses, following a mild TSH rise four weeks earlier; dostarlimab continued</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E03.2</td><td>S — Synthetic common immune-related adverse event of PD-1 blockade.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

